61,3,,status,Victoria,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":4,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
62,3,,status,Shunichi,,ALIVE,"{""seat"":5,""role"":""PARTICIPANT"",""alive"":true}"
63,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"Looking at the options, Plan D addresses the obsolete equipment @Shunichi mentioned and benefits shared labs, which aligns with my findings on user satisfaction. However, we must check if it causes the room displacement @Victoria fears.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Shunichi"",""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"Plan D is the most risky regarding displacement. Upgrading ventilation and layouts usually requires closing labs entirely. Given our lack of buffer space, the off-site rental costs for teaching labs would be astronomical.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Plan D is a textbook example of a project that will slip. It involves mechanical plant—which @Kenji noted is rising in cost—and is essential, yet complex. We would be inviting a budgetary catastrophe.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,1,talk,Kenji,,"@Benjamin is right! Plan D has all that mechanical stuff that's getting way more expensive. If we pick that, we might run out of money before the labs are even finished! What about Plan A?","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,1,talk,Shunichi,,"@Kenji, Plan A is just a fancy lounge. It does nothing for the people actually working. Plan D is the only one that stops our technicians from acting like human machinery. We can't keep ignoring the labor cost.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Kenji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@Shunichi, I understand the urgency, but we cannot ignore the risk of total failure. If Plan D causes a budget collapse or halts teaching, it helps no one. Plan B offers shared utility and modern equipment without the same structural risks.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"Plan B also risks displacement if it converts existing rooms, but it is less invasive than Plan D. However, Plan A utilizes an underused hall, meaning it creates capacity without taking any active rooms out of service.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Plan A is the only option that avoids the ""displacement trap"" @Victoria identified. It transforms an underused space rather than disrupting active ones, making it the only logically sound choice for our current constraints.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,2,talk,Kenji,,"I agree with @Benjamin and @Victoria! Plan A uses a space that's already underused, so it won't cost us extra for off-site rentals. Plus, it doesn't have the expensive mechanical plant costs of Plan D!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Benjamin, your ""logic"" is just a convenient way to avoid doing anything difficult. Plan A is a cosmetic band-aid. If we prioritize a lounge over the people struggling in the labs, we're just polishing the deck chairs.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@Shunichi, I hear you, but we must be pragmatic. Plan A provides a shared resource that users actually value, without the hazardous material risks of ground-floor partitions or the budget volatility of Plan D. It is the safest, most beneficial choice.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""Shunichi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"I must agree with @Mizuki. While Plan D is necessary in the long term, our current timetable cannot absorb the loss of labs. Plan A is the only option that improves the campus without risking a financial and operational crisis.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a rare pleasure to see us in such agreement. Plan A is the only candidate that respects both the budget and the calendar, avoiding the inevitable delays of more ambitious, ""comfort-driven"" delusions.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,3,talk,Kenji,,"I'm with you all! Plan A is the smartest move because it's realistic. It doesn't risk the huge price jumps I saw in the tender records, and it won't leave us broke! Let's go with Plan A!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,3,talk,Shunichi,,"You've all managed to reach a consensus on the most cowardly option. Fine. If you're more comfortable with a fancy lounge than fixing a broken system, so be it. I'll vote for D, but don't come crying to me when the labs fail.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,"It is a difficult choice, but we must prioritize the stability of the university's operations. Plan A is the only one that guarantees a positive outcome without risking a systemic collapse. I vote for Plan A.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Victoria,,"I must prioritize the operational reality of our timetable. Since Plan A creates new capacity without displacing existing services or incurring off-site costs, it is the only responsible choice. I vote for Plan A.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
80,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"Given the evidence, any other choice would be a gamble with the calendar and the budget. I prefer a predictable success over a romanticized disaster. I vote for Plan A.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
81,3,4,talk,Kenji,,I'm voting for Plan A too! It's the only one that doesn't risk those scary price jumps or hidden costs. Let's pick something we can actually afford to finish! I vote for Plan A.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
82,3,4,talk,Shunichi,,"I'll vote for Plan D. I refuse to endorse a plan that treats staff exhaustion as an acceptable cost of ""stability."" Enjoy your lounge while the labs crumble around you. I vote for Plan D.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
